{%- set churchIntro -%}<p class="page-intro">St Peter's serves Abbots Morton, a few miles north of Harvington, and gives the ARCH Benefice its A. The abbots of Evesham once kept a country retreat in the village, and the moat of their manor house can still be traced near the church. It is listed Grade I.</p>{%- endset -%}
{%- set churchAbout -%}<h2>The church through the centuries</h2>
    <p>A Saxon church once stood on the site. The present building's oldest walls are 12th century; the chancel was rebuilt and widened in the 14th century, when a north chapel was also added (it now holds the organ and choir). The tower dates from around 1400 and the timber-framed porch from soon after. Abbots Morton's list of rectors starts with Petrus le Meare in 1288.</p>
    <h2>Things to see</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The roof: its 15th-century beams are mostly branches, barely shaped. In 1955 the parish raised more than £1,300 to preserve them.</li>
      <li>The porch, with two 15th-century pews, and the south door, now the only entrance.</li>
      <li>Four bells: a 15th-century "Royal Head" tenor whose inscription is marked with the heads of Edward III and Queen Philippa, one of 1633, and two of 1842 by Thomas Mears of London.</li>
      <li>The tower, restored in 2015 with Chicksgrove stone after the parish raised £45,000.</li>
    </ul>{%- endset -%}
{%- set servicesIntro -%}<p>Services at St Peter's come round on some Sundays, in turn with the other ARCH churches. Coming up:</p>{%- endset -%}
